regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:1:I'll start from the repo and the LCA config path, then check the tag-shift premise against source rather than ratifying the sketch.Checkpoint is behind HEAD (`4b91d7a`); I'll read the LCA config handler, tag layout, and `bus_pci` from the current tree.Guest OpenBSD LCA sources are in-tree; I'll read those against `lca_pci_conf` before trusting the tag-shift sketch.OpenBSD’s `lca_conf_read` treats `IOC_CONF` as cycle type, not bus number. I’ll check the probe’s armed address and how other PCI hosts decode type-1 tags.**VERDICT: IMPLEMENT, but the sketch is one assignment short.** `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the right extract of *this* `tag` (Q1 is not a double-shift). Also assign `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` — `ioc_conf & 1` is the PCI cycle *type*, not the bus number. Member 7; fall through into `bus_pci`. The proposed all-ones row is necessary and not sufficient: the witness address is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:5:I read `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` and `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` in `GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c`, `bus_pci_setaddr` / `bus_pci_data_access` / `bus_pci_decompose_1` in `bus_pci.c`, all **10** `bus_pci_setaddr` call sites under `GXEMUL-SEC/src/` (lca, uninorth, sh4, eagle, cpc700, gt, footbridge, i80321, sgi_ip32, v3), `DEVINIT(lca)`, `PCIINIT(i82378zb)`, `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, and the in-tree OpenBSD 2.2 driver (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c`, `lca.c`, `lcareg.h`). I did not run a gate.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:48:Three independent sources, none of them the model’s own `lca_ioc` comment:
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:56:The `lca_ioc` comment (“PCI bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit”) is the model author’s collapse of those two quantities. Shipping the sketch *and* leaving that comment is a wrong record: the sketch still treats the bit as the bus number, which is what the comment says and what the header/OpenBSD contradict.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:125:- `lca_ioc` `default:` (`STAT0`/`STAT1`/`SFT_RST`/…). Master-abort reporting through the register the header designates for it stays blocked. The `lcaconfbus1kill` row already says so.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:129:- `lcabadval`’s other IOC value guards.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:131:Correct the `lca_ioc` IOC_CONF comment in the same diff if you take bus from the tag; otherwise the comment and the code agree on a false picture.
regress\artifacts\lcaconfbus1kill\pass1_panel\grok.txt:138:2. **OpenBSD says IOC_CONF bits `<1:0>`**, two bits, write-only (`lca.c` §6.4.2). The model accepts only bit 0 (`idata & ~1`) and kills on bit 1. Out of scope (that is `lcabadval`); do not widen the accept set as a rider.
